{{Short description|History and collapse of the petroleum extraction industry in Balisca}}{{Economy of Balisca}}
The '''petroleum industry in Balisca''' encompassed the exploration, extraction, refining, and distribution of [[petroleum|crude oil]] and petroleum products within the [[Baliscan Confederation]] from the early 20th century until its permanent collapse during the [[Special Period]] (1976–1983). At its peak in the early 1970s, the industry supplied approximately 60% of Balisca's domestic petroleum needs and constituted a significant sector of the national economy. 

Oil production was a minor factor in the 19th century, with [[W:Kerosene|kerosene]] replacing [[W:Whale oil|whale oil]] and [[W:Lubricants|lubricants]] becoming essential to the [[W:Machine age|machine age]]. Oil became a major [[Economy of Balisca|Baliscan industry]] in the 20th century with the discovery on new fields around [[Canyars]] and the [[Valley|NAME valley]], and the dramatic increase in demand for [[W:Gasoline|gasoline]] to fuel automobiles and trucks. In 1900 Estuaria pumped {{convert|4|e6oilbbl|m3}}, nearly 5% of the national supply. Then came a series of major discoveries, and the community pumped {{convert|100|e6oilbbl|e6m3|abbr=unit}} in 1914, or 68% of the national supply. In 1970, Estuaria produced {{convert|597|e6oilbbl|e6m3|abbr=unit|sigfig=2}} of [[W:Crude oil|crude oil]], out of the total {{convert|975|e6oilbbl|e6m3|abbr=unit|sigfig=3}} of oil produced in Balisca, representing 53.3% of national production.

There was also significant [[Offshore oil and gas in Balisca|offshore oil and gas production in Balisca]], but in 1968 the Confederal Congress adopted a moratorium on new offshore oil and gas leasing and new [[W:Oil platforms|oil platforms]] in both Estuaria and confederal waters. These restrictions were imposed after the [[1969 Santa Bàrbara oil spill]] released oil into the [[Garrigàs Lake|Garrigàs Gulf]].

The [[Marañón oil fires]] (1976–1982), combined with wartime disruption and sustained indigenous-environmental opposition, rendered the industry permanently defunct by the early 1990s. The [[Petroleum Phase-Out Initiative]] (1993) formally ended all petroleum-related activities in Balisca. The industry's collapse represents a defining case in [[W:Energy transition|energy transition]] studies, demonstrating how environmental catastrophe, indigenous sovereignty movements, and coordinated social opposition can permanently dismantle an established extractive sector. Contemporary Balisca maintains no petroleum production, having transitioned entirely to [[W:Renewable energy|renewable energy]], [[W:Nuclear power|nuclear power]] for its energy needs and [[W:Bioethanol|bioethanol]] for fuel consumption.

== History ==

=== Discovery and early development ===
[[File:Fotothek df fb-pos 0000281 Öltürme auf Signal Hill bei Lo.jpg|left|thumb|Exploratory [[W:Oil well|oil wells]] built on the the coast of [[Quiscosta]], near [[Gramenet de Daimús]], following the discovery of oil in 1914]]
Petroleum was first discovered in Balisca in 1913, when exploration teams commissioned by the government of [[tba]] identified surface seepages in the [[Marañón River|Marañón]] basin near the border between [[Estuaria]] and border. Initial extraction began in 1918, with modest production intended primarily for Baliscan naval needs as the country was engaged in the [[Algarvean naval arms race]] with [[Potocsí]] and [[Sanrobonia]].

Following the [[Baliscan Revolution]] (1918–1922), the nascent petroleum industry became a point of intense ideological conflict, with statists calling for the industry to be centralized as an "[[essential industry]]". The 1923 [[Decree on Mineral Resources]] nationalized all petroleum deposits and established the [[Baliscan Oil Corporation]] (COBAL), a worker-managed entity under confederal oversight. The revolutionary settlement represented a distinctive approach: petroleum extraction would continue, but under cooperative ownership with significant revenue allocation to affected communities. This arrangement proved insufficient to resolve fundamental tensions between the industry and the [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous communities]] whose historic territories hosted the oil fields.

=== Expansion and peak production ===

The industry expanded substantially from the 1930s through the 1960s, driven by the electrification of Baliscan cities, the growing automobile fleet, and wartime needs following the Baliscan entry into the [[Great Adonian War]]. The [[Marañón oil field|Marañón field complex]]—spanning the border between Estuaria and Pindorama—became the primary production zone, with additional discoveries in the coastal waters of eastern Estuaria and inland [[Bayara]].

