Peak production in 1972 reached approximately 340,000 barrels per day, supplying roughly 60% of Baliscan domestic consumption. The remaining demand was met through imports from [[Kaftia|Kaftian]] producers, primarily via the [[Sanyan]] terminal facilities.

=== Social and environmental tensions ===

Throughout its operational history, the petroleum industry generated sustained opposition from multiple quarters:

* '''Indigenous communities''' in [[Cerdanya]], [[Pindorama]] and [[Estuaria]] contested the terms under which oil extraction occurred on their territories, arguing that the 1923 nationalization and subsequent arrangements had been imposed without adequate consultation or consent.
* '''Environmental movements''' documented groundwater contamination, [[W:Oil spill|spills]], and ecological damage associated with extraction and transport.
* '''Labor syndicates''' within the COBAL raised concerns about worker safety and long-term health impacts, particularly for workers in the Marañón field complex.
* '''Political movements''' associated with [[Ecological Action]] and anti-extractivist tendencies articulated ideological opposition to petroleum extraction as inconsistent with Baliscan [[W:Libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist]] values.

These tensions intensified during the 1960s and early 1970s as production expanded and environmental impacts became more visible. The 1968 Kwaray oil spill in the Marañón River—estimated at 40,000 barrels—crystallized opposition and prompted the first large-scale indigenous-environmental alliance targeting the industry.

=== Aborted revival and collapse ===

{{main|Marañón oil fires|Special Period}}

The petroleum industry's collapse resulted from the convergence of the [[Camboriú War]] (1976–1979), the [[Bight of Ayorin Crisis]], and the catastrophic [[Marañón oil fires]]. In September 1976, with Baliscan military resources committed to the war front, the [[Potocsí|Potocsian]] military conducted airstrikes on the Marañón field complex, igniting wellheads and storage facilities. Sabotage by [[Ayorin]]-aligned militias compounded the damage in early 1977.

The resulting fires burned for over eighteen months, releasing an estimated 45 million barrels of oil into the environment—contaminating the Marañón River watershed, destroying agricultural land, and causing long-term health impacts for exposed populations. Firefighting efforts were constrained by wartime resource limitations and the scale of the disaster.

Production collapsed from 185,000 barrels per day in late 1976 to under 22,000 by 1980, with most surviving infrastructure inoperable due to fire damage, spill contamination, or infrastructure disruption. The industry had effectively ceased to function as a productive entity by 1983, though small-scale extraction continued in isolated fields through the mid-1980s.

== Geography and infrastructure ==

=== Oil fields ===

[[File:Marañón oil field map.png|thumb|250px|Map of the Marañón field complex showing the extent of infrastructure and the fire zones (1976)]]

The primary petroleum reserves of Balisca were located in the [[Marañón basin]], on the border between [[Estuaria]] and [[Pindorama]]. This geological formation, in the [[Marañón River|Marañón river]] watershed, contained approximately 85% of Balisca's proven petroleum reserves.

Major fields within the Marañón complex included:

